<Base
  title="User Guide — Ekphrasis"
  description="How to use Ekphrasis: the ink curve, reading paths, frequency axis, quality, voices, and spectral transcription."
>
  <h1>User Guide</h1>
  <p>
    Ekphrasis reads an image as a spectrogram and resynthesizes it into
    sound in real time. A reading head sweeps the picture in musical time;
    each row of pixels is a frequency band, each column a moment, and how
    bright a pixel is decides how loudly that band sounds. The result runs
    through Griffin-Lim phase reconstruction, which is what makes the
    output recognizably follow the picture rather than merely being
    triggered by it.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide covers what the plugin does once it's loaded and an image is
    on the display. For installing, finding it in your host, and
    uninstalling, see the <a href="/ekphrasis/manual">Manual</a>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="quick-start">Quick start</h2>
  <img src="/media/ekphrasis-guide-ui.png" alt="The Ekphrasis editor with an image loaded, the reading path and playhead drawn over it" />
  <p>
    Load an image by dropping it on the display, or with the
    <strong>Load Image…</strong> button. <strong>Hold a note — or tick
    Hold.</strong> With no note held, Ekphrasis is silent even while the
    transport runs; Hold latches a note at the Transpose Root with no
    controller, no armed track, and no MIDI routing at all — the fastest
    way to audition a picture.
  </p>
  <p>Two things commonly explain silence, and neither is a fault:</p>
  <ul>
    <li>JUCE's standalone opens <strong>no MIDI input on macOS</strong> until you enable one in Options → Audio/MIDI Settings.</li>
    <li>An <strong>unarmed track</strong> in your host passes no notes to the plugin.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    The status row at the bottom of the editor always says which of these
    applies — <em>"Silent: no image"</em>, <em>"Silent: no note held (or
    Hold)"</em>, or <em>"Sounding: C3"</em>.
  </p>

  <h2 id="display-ink-curve">The display &amp; ink curve</h2>
  <img src="/media/ekphrasis-guide-ink-curve.png" alt="The ink controls with Threshold raised, showing the image visibly darkened" />
  <p>
    What you see on the display is the <strong>inked</strong> image — the
    ink curve applied, exactly what the engine reads. Three controls shape
    it: <strong>Invert</strong> flips which end of the brightness range
    counts as loud, <strong>Threshold</strong> darkens the picture and
    silences quiet bands together, and <strong>Contrast</strong> steepens
    or flattens the mapping from brightness to level. Watching the display,
    not the source file, is the fastest way to understand what a given
    image will sound like.
  </p>

  <h2 id="reading-paths-pacing">Reading paths &amp; pacing</h2>
  <img src="/media/ekphrasis-guide-reading-paths.svg" alt="Four reading paths swept over the same image, with the row/column/brightness mapping" />
  <p>
    The <strong>Path</strong> parameter chooses how the reading head sweeps
    the image: horizontal, vertical, radial, or spiral. <strong>Pacing</strong>
    shapes how the head moves through that path over time: even,
    accelerando, rallentando, or breathing.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Scan Time</strong> sets how long one full traversal takes, and
    its unit depends on <strong>Host Sync</strong>: bars when synced to
    your host's transport, seconds when free-running. The panel shows both
    the unit and the resolved length in seconds.
  </p>

  <h2 id="frequency-axis">Frequency axis</h2>
  <img src="/media/ekphrasis-guide-frequency-axis.png" alt="The frequency panel with Log axis, a narrowed Min/Max range, and a musical scale engaged" />
  <p>
    <strong>Min Hz</strong> and <strong>Max Hz</strong> set the frequency
    range the image is read across. <strong>Axis</strong> chooses
    logarithmic or linear mapping from image row to frequency — logarithmic
    (the default) matches how pitch is perceived. <strong>Scale</strong>
    and <strong>Scale Root</strong> optionally quantize the frequency axis
    to a musical scale, so every row lands on a scale tone instead of a
    continuous frequency.
  </p>

  <h2 id="spectral-quality">Spectral quality</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Quality</strong> sets how many Griffin-Lim iterations
    reconstruct each block: Draft (8), Normal (32), or High (96). More
    iterations mean a closer resemblance to the image and more CPU work per
    block. <strong>Tonal ↔ Airy</strong> shapes the spectral character
    independent of iteration count. High quality halves the available
    polyphony: eight voices at Draft or Normal, but only two at High.
  </p>

  <h2 id="voice-polyphony">Voice &amp; polyphony</h2>
  <img src="/media/ekphrasis-guide-voice-quality-panel.png" alt="The voice and quality control panel with Transpose Root shifted and Quality set to High" />
  <p>
    <strong>Transpose Root</strong> sets which note sounds the image
    untransposed. <strong>Attack</strong> and <strong>Release</strong>
    shape each note's envelope; both apply immediately. Ekphrasis is
    polyphonic: up to eight held notes (two at High quality) each transpose
    the spectrum independently, and an MPE controller gives each note its
    own pitch bend and pressure. A ninth note steals the oldest.
    <strong>A cold transposition costs one block</strong> — a note at a
    transposition not yet rendered waits for the renderer before it sounds.
  </p>

  <h2 id="transcription">Transcription</h2>
  <img src="/media/ekphrasis-guide-transcription-on.png" alt="The transcription controls active, with Transcribe Voices and Transcribe Thresh both off their defaults" />
  <p>
    Turn <strong>Transcribe</strong> on to send the picture out as MIDI
    notes. The engine tracks the loudest partials in the spectrum it is
    already rendering and emits each one as an MPE note.
    <strong>Transcribe Voices</strong> sets how many partials may sound as
    notes at once; <strong>Transcribe Thresh</strong> sets how loud a
    partial has to be before it becomes a note. It's off by default, emits
    whenever the transport runs, and always reads the untransposed image.
  </p>
  <p>
    To receive the notes in Live, set a second track's <strong>MIDI
    From</strong> to the Ekphrasis track — the <strong>VST3</strong> is the
    only format Live offers as a routing source. Two settings are required
    and neither is the default: the chooser under <em>MIDI From</em> must
    read <code>Ekphrasis</code>, not <code>Post FX</code>, and
    <strong>MPE must be enabled on the Ekphrasis device</strong> itself, or
    Live folds every note onto one channel and discards the per-note
    bends. See the <a href="/ekphrasis/manual">Manual</a> for install and
    general routing setup.
  </p>

  <h2 id="midi-thru-panic">MIDI Thru &amp; Panic</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>MIDI Thru</strong> passes incoming MIDI back out, and like
    transcription's output, it's only routable from the VST3 in Live.
    <strong>Panic</strong> releases every sounding voice and closes every
    MPE note the plugin has emitted — the way out if a note-off is ever
    lost in transit and a note is left stuck on.
  </p>

  <h2 id="parameters">Parameters reference</h2>
  <table>
    <tr><th>Group</th><th>Parameters</th></tr>
    <tr><td>Ink</td><td>Invert, Threshold, Contrast</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Reading</td><td>Path, Pacing, Scan Time (bars when synced, seconds when free)</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Frequency</td><td>Min Hz, Max Hz, Axis (log/linear), Scale, Scale Root</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Spectral</td><td>Quality (Draft / Normal / High), Tonal ↔ Airy</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Voice</td><td>Transpose Root, Attack, Release</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Output</td><td>Spectral Gain, Master</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Sync</td><td>Host Sync, Hold</td></tr>
    <tr><td>MIDI</td><td>Bend Range, MIDI Thru</td></tr>
    <tr><td>Transcription</td><td>Transcribe, Transcribe Voices, Transcribe Threshold</td></tr>
  </table>
  <p>
    These are the names your host shows. <em>Transcribe Threshold</em> is
    labelled <strong>Transcribe Thresh</strong> in the editor panel, because
    the full name would be truncated at the editor's minimum width.
  </p>

  <h2 id="about-panel">About panel &amp; credits</h2>
  <img src="/media/ekphrasis-guide-about-panel.png" alt="The About panel showing the version and build identifier" />
  <p>
    The About panel shows the installed version and build identifier — the
    same number your host reports.
  </p>

